      "Key": "seduce",
      "Title": "Seduce, don\u0027t just observe",
      "Status": "warn",
      "Evidence": "The opening green-room texture (chain lamp, taped cable rivers, Mona\u0027s light-strip pulsing amber to white, Rill\u0027s wrist-seam dampeners, Dayo\u0027s poet-naming of Glass/Fade/Candor) is genuinely seductive lived-in-wrong detail that pulls before horror lands \u2014 but once the Candor kicks in and the telepathy/horror machinery starts, the prose shifts almost entirely into thriller-mechanics mode (turns counted, read-outputs relayed like tactical comms) and stops finding new strange-beautiful textures to seduce with.",
      "Fix": "Thread at least one or two more sensory/world-texture beats (the genemod fangs, the Thread space-elevator line, the neuretics handshake) into the escape and shootout sections so the seduction continues alongside the horror instead of yielding the floor entirely to plot mechanics."
    },
    {
      "Key": "open_grounded",
      "Title": "Open with the world doing its work",
      "Status": "fail",
      "Evidence": "The story opens mid-adrenaline, immediately after a set: \u0027The last chord was still hanging out front when Ethan Wolfe pushed through the green room door\u0027 \u2014 the plot\u0027s emotional engine (exhaustion, the Mona tension, the imminent Dayo deal) is already running in paragraph one, with no grounded, plotless entry beat of a stranger-eyed world before the character-and-stakes machinery engages.",
      "Fix": "Precede this with a short beat of ordinary world-texture untouched by plot \u2014 e.g., a bouncer counting a till, a bartender restocking, the city\u0027s mundane commerce outside the club \u2014 before dropping into Ethan\u0027s post-show nerves and the band\u0027s charged dynamics."
    },
    {
      "Key": "gloss_in_voice",
      "Title": "Gloss first mentions in-voice, never dump",
      "Status": "warn",
      "Evidence": "Most proprietary nouns (neuretics, CorpoNations-adjacent brand names like Waxwing Neuromedia, Fenris Ballistics, Greymark Unseen, The Low, The Thread) are dropped in via appositive or sensory action without lecturing \u2014 e.g. \u0027his overlay ran empty\u0027 and \u0027the Thread\u0027s pods barely moved against the dark, the space elevator holding its long thin line down\u0027 gloss geography cleanly in-voice. But some terms land as unglossed drops that assume prior context, like \u0027the dampeners in his wrists\u0027 and \u0027Rill went inward and the drums came out\u0027 for Glass, plus \u0027Zone 4\u0027 and \u0027Zone-4 Umami\u0027 used as if the reader already has the zone map, which risks losing a true first-timer.",
      "Fix": "Add one small in-voice anchor the first time \u0027Zone 4\u0027 or \u0027The Low\u0027 appears (e.g. a short sensory or status cue showing what living in that zone means) so newcomers get their footing without a lecture."
    },
    {
